From 514039150fcadfd15685565ca47f56427decf242 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:10:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] expand: `stringify!` of a forwarded fragment prints no
 binding/hygiene comments, a block fragment survives whole

libz-ng-sys@1.1.28 / libz-sys@1.1.28 (hck 0.11.6 via flate2 1.1.9, zlib-ng): "Undefined symbols for architecture ppc:
_zng_, referenced from: ...flate2...ffi::c::Inflate::decompress_inner" (ld's suggestions: _zng_inflate,
_zng_inflateEnd, ...). Every zlib import is declared `#[link_name = zng_prefix!(inflate)] pub fn inflate(..);` with
`macro_rules! zng_prefix { ($name:expr) => { concat!("zng_", stringify!($name)) } }` (`stringify!($name)` alone
without cfg(zng)). `stringify!` printed the `expr` fragment through DumpAST_Node, whose path printer is the debug one:
the link name was `zng_/*1*/inflate/*?*/` (hygiene + binding comments). GNU as strips the comments, so Linux links
worked; Apple's assembler takes `_zng_`. 0304 had kept these comments as "harmless to the lexer", which holds for
proc-macro input but not for a string used as data (link names, export names, assert messages).

ast/dump.cpp: RustPrinter has a debug flag, on only for Dump_Rust. Off (DumpAST_Node/DumpAST_Pattern: stringify!,
proc-macro input, re-captured fragments, all of which re-lex the text or use it as data) it prints paths and types
without comments, names without hygiene, no binding slots, labels by name, and named pattern values through the same
path printer. ast/path.cpp: Path::print_pretty without is_debug also prints the generic arguments (types, lifetimes,
associated type bindings) and a qualified path's type and trait without comments (they always went through the debug
operator<<); the debug output is unchanged. expand/stringify.cpp: the strip of a leading `{..}` (from hygiene printed
in braces, `/*..*/` since upstream 102b9e3e, 2024-02) cut a `$b:block` fragment down to its last line (`\n}`).

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context)
